WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.369
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:04.904
who you know
from "Difficult People,"

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:07.740
"American Horror Story"
and "Billy on the Street."

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.510
He co-wrote and stars
in the new movie "Bros,"

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.645
which premieres in theaters
September 30th.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:13.946
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:15.948
-I woke up laughing about
you so rudely calling me out

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:16.783
on my [bleep].

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.350
-Well, you deserved it, bitch.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.652
Honestly, I was impressed.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.787
You may be more emotionally
unavailable than I am.

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:23.790
-Well, maybe we can be
emotionally unavailable

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.091
together.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.993
-Maybe we can be emotionally
unavailable together?

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:29.395
Who's writing your texts,
Maroon 5?

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:30.396
-[Bleep] off.

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:31.964
-Kidding. We can go out.

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:33.199
Are you asking me out?

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.068
-I'm down for whatever.
-Yeah, same.

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:36.269
-Cool.
-Sounds good.

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.171
-So, tomorrow?
Or we can do whenever.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.272
-Yeah, I can do whenever
and I can do whatever.

00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:42.074
-I don't care what we do.
-Yeah, me neither.

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:44.177
We can do whatever
and we can do it whenever.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:46.012
Does that work for you?
-Yeah, that definitely works.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.648
-Great. Whatever, whenever.
-Cool. Whatever, whenever.

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.550
Gif of Michael Scott dancing.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.551
That's good.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:52.551
-"Office" gif?

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:53.987
This person isn't gay.

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:55.388
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:58.057
-Please welcome back to
the show, our very good friend,

00:00:58.057 --> 00:00:59.659
the one, the only Billy Eichner,
everybody.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.662
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:07.667 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:12.638 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:15.141
-Billy, welcome back!
-What's up, New York?!

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.878
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.413
Hi.
-What an exciting time

00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:22.948
to be sitting here next to you.

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:24.350
You co-wrote this film.
-Yeah.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.485
-You star in this film.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:28.754
-And this then is a rom-com
where, you know,

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:30.490
the leads are two gay men.
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.060
-Give us a little synopsis for
anybody who might not know.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.263
-"Bros" is the first
gay rom-com ever released

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:39.298
by a major studio.

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.004
-Yeah, and it's also produced

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.706
by Judd Apatow and co-written
with Nick Stoler.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.009
Those guys have made some
of the funniest movies ever --

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.011
"Bridesmaids,"
and "40-year-Old Virgin,"

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.046
and "Knocked Up,"
and "Train Wreck,"

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:54.347
and "Forgetting Sarah Marshall."

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.916
But, this is the first time
they've ever made a movie

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.651
like that,
but about a gay couple.

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.186
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:01.820
Yeah.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.059
And it is long overdue,
but I'm so excited about it.

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:08.561
And it's basically about --
I play Bobby Leiber,

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.629
who's a podcaster
at the beginning of the movie

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:12.865
and then gets a job working on
the board of what will be

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:15.968
the first LGBTQ history museum
in New York City,

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.704
which leads to a lot of
infighting among various members

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:21.006
of my community.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:21.006 --> 00:02:23.876
And then Bobby's a guy
who prides himself

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.177
on not needing anyone,

00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:27.713
not wanting to be in a romantic
relationship.

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:30.349
And then he meets Aaron,
who he was texting with,

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:31.917
played by the wonderful
Luke McFarland --

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:33.753
-He's fantastic in the film.
-Who's so good in the movie.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.321
-And they're both guys,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.489
they've have been out
their whole lives,

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.656
they're very sexually active,

00:02:37.656 --> 00:02:39.192
but both of them --
it is almost like

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.060
they're in a competition to see
who can be

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.730
more emotionally unavailable.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:44.930
-[ Laughs ]
-Right?

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.932
And, though --
But they fall for each other.

00:02:46.932 --> 00:02:48.367
They fall in love unexpectedly.

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:51.003
And we watch these two guys
who really pride themselves

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:52.938
on not needing love
fall in love

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:55.875
and what happens when they have
to let their guard down.

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.677
-You know, I was telling you
backstage,

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:00.946
it works so wonderfully as
a super funny rom-com.

00:03:00.946 --> 00:03:02.281
-Yeah, thank you.
-Like, it has all

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:03.749
of the classic elements
you love.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:05.652
And then, every now and then,
there's moments

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:08.787
where you remember
that it's also historic

00:03:08.787 --> 00:03:10.756
in the fact
that it's covering something

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:12.057
that's never been covered.

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:13.459
You've been doing screenings
all over the country.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.127
-Yeah.
-You went to TIFF.

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.664
At those screenings,
do you feel the audience

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:18.697
is having that reaction as well?

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:19.932
It must be so important
to people.

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:21.300
-It's -- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.536
TIFF is the Toronto
International Film Festival,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.571
which usually doesn't
take romantic comedies

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.006
like this, you know?
-Yeah.

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:29.442
-And that was amazing. And
what's also incredible is that,

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.611
you know, Nick and I --
you don't sit down

00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:33.612
to write a historic scene.
-Sure.

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:35.381
-You don't sit down to write
a gay movie.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.717
We just wanted to make
a hilarious movie.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.386
And think what we've forgotten
partially because of COVID --

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.855
it's just, we don't get
many comedies like this

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.791
in a movie theater anymore,
you know?

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.525
I don't know why.
I grew up loving

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.828
"When Harry Met Sally,"
and "Moonstruck,"

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:52.598
and "Sleepless in Seattle"
and "You've Got Mail"

00:03:52.598 --> 00:03:55.234
and, you know,
all these great movies,

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:58.637
and I think people have
forgotten how much fun

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.773
it is to sit in a movie
theater with hundreds

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.608
of other people
and laugh together.

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:06.379
You know, it really is --
it's a feel-good movie and --

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:07.880
that's such
a joyful experience,

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.483
and I'm so glad "Bros" will give
that to people again.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.987
-And having written it,
I'm assuming you pulled a lot

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.821
from your own
personal experiences.

00:04:15.821 --> 00:04:18.257
What were things from your
own life that were important

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:20.025
for you to get into the script?

00:04:20.025 --> 00:04:22.461
-Oh, God. I mean, all that
passive aggressive stuff

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:23.829
over texting.
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:04:23.829 --> 00:04:25.697
-You know, I mean, this is what
I mean.

00:04:25.697 --> 00:04:27.800
Like, we've had audiences
who are mostly straight

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.934
watch the movie.

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:30.369
We've had audiences
that are more LGBTQ,

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:33.005
but these are things
anyone who's ever been single

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.174
in the past 15 years
can relate to.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.476
-Yeah.
-You know, because dating apps

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:41.046
and texting and sexting --
it's driving everyone crazy.

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:44.150
Because it's allowed everyone
to be passive aggressive

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.585
and noncommittal,

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:48.888
and it's driven me insane many
times with guys like the one

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:50.155
that Luke plays in the movie.

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:51.691
-But this --
Is this true, that you've got --

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:53.960
you've been kicked off
Tinder twice?

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.229
-Yes, Seth, I have.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:00.833
-I not only -- I'm not only the
maker of the first gay rom-com

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:01.900
from a major studio,

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.903
I've also been kicked off
of Tinder twice.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.137
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:06.138
-Yeah.
-Which is hard to do.

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:08.507
They're pretty forgiving.
-Let me --

00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:10.143
-[ Laughs ]
-Let me explain why.

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.410
I didn't do anything wrong.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:12.878
You know,
I'm like everyone else.

00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:15.181
I'm just trying to be a normal
person, like, meeting people.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:17.816
So I join Tinder,
but then people recognize me.

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:19.151
They don't think it's me.

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:21.821
They think it's someone
pretending to be me, right?

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.355
So, they report my account.

00:05:23.355 --> 00:05:25.324
And Tinder thought they were
doing the right thing

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:27.526
and they booted me twice.

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:29.628
And then I complained
about it,

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:31.964
and then they sent me
a care package.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:38.004
Imagine how depressing it is

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.706
when you're
a 44-year-old single man

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:42.942
getting a care package
from Tinder.

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.110
-Yeah.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.211
-That had literally a T-shirt
that said

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:47.980
"World's Greatest Single."

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.850
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:54.854
There was like a mug that was
like "Single, But Loving It."

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:56.022
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.589
And, yeah, with, like --
and by the way, you know,

00:05:57.589 --> 00:05:58.991
shout out to Tinder.

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:01.059
The thought was very nice,
but it was honestly

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:03.529
the most depressing gift
I've ever received.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:04.963
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.963 --> 00:06:08.134
And "Bros" very much draws on
experiences like that.

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:10.002
-I have a lot more to
ask you.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.206
We will be right back with more
from Billy Eichner after this.

